.docname {Plugin Capabilities}
.docauthor {BakingRL}
.include {docs}
.doclang {en}

This page is the capability inventory for BakingRL plugin packages. Use it to
decide what a package should expose, which host APIs it needs, and which
permissions must appear in `bakingrl.plugin.json`.

The SDK types the authoring contract. The BakingRL host owns installation,
runtime compatibility, permission enforcement, page and overlay rendering,
service startup, network access, registry access, and package configuration.

## Package-Level Contract

Every package is described by `bakingrl.plugin.json`.

Core v2 fields:

- `schema`: must be `bakingrl.plugin/2`.
- `id`: stable package identity, usually reverse-DNS style.
- `name`, `version`, and optional `author`: package display and versioning
  metadata.
- `compatibility.runtimeApi`: the host runtime API the package targets.
- `exports`: capabilities provided by the package.
- `imports`: components and services consumed from other packages.
- `permissions`: host-mediated access requested by package code.
- `settings`: package-level settings JSON Schema.

Trusted extension packages use `schema: "bakingrl.plugin/3"` and
`kind: "trusted"` instead of v2 `exports`. Their runtime surface is declared
with `runtime.extensionHost.entry`, `activation.events`,
`runtime.sidecars`, `contributes.webviews`, and `capabilities.permissions`.

Generated packages also contain Vite inputs, TypeScript source files, package
helper scripts, and built `dist/` entries that must match the manifest.

## Executable Exports

`exports.visuals`

Browser ESM modules mounted inside overlays, OBS browser-source layouts, custom
pages, and private configuration pages. A visual receives a host-owned DOM
`root`, instance metadata, merged settings, and read-oriented frontend APIs.
Visuals can subscribe to permitted telemetry or plugin events, read permitted
registry keys, load components, call permitted services, resolve assets, and
report diagnostics.

Visuals can also expose an editor contract:

- `editor.mount(context)`: optional editor-only setup.
- `editor.actions(context)`: optional buttons shown by the host editor.
- `context.editor.emit(eventName, payload)`: injects a local event into that
  visual instance only.

Editor events are not published to the live host bus. They are meant for
showing representative visual states and manual triggers while designing a
page or layout.

`exports.components`

Reusable browser DOM fragments loaded by visuals through
`context.components.load(ref)`. Components are not global UI widgets; they are
mounted into a root provided by the consuming visual and should treat props as
untrusted input.

`exports.services`

Sandboxed backend modules. Services can subscribe to telemetry and plugin
events, publish permitted plugin events, read and write permitted registry
keys, read and write permitted text storage, call permitted services, read
package settings, and expose RPC methods to visuals or other services.

Use services for derived state, aggregation, long-lived backend state, and
runtime coordination that should not live inside one visual instance.

`exports.connectors`

Backend modules with the service APIs plus host-mediated HTTP, WebSocket, and
secret access. Connectors are for external systems. They do not expose RPC
methods directly in the current SDK contract; pair a connector with a service
when other plugin code needs callable methods.

## Declarative Exports

`exports.pages`

Importable custom page templates. Importing a page template creates a
host-owned page that users can edit like any other page. The package does not
own a route or standalone window.

`exports.layouts`

Importable overlay layout templates. Users can route imported layouts to the
in-game overlay or OBS stream output. Template IDs and item IDs can be rewritten
during import, so portable behavior must live in package/export references and
item settings.

`exports.configuration`

One private fixed-size package configuration page opened from package details.
Its private visuals are not listed in the normal visual picker and should not
be referenced from public pages or layouts. The host renders configuration
pages on a `1200x740` canvas and keeps secret fields in host-owned UI.

`exports.assets`

Package-owned static files. Visuals and components should resolve files through
`context.assets.url(ref)` instead of using local filesystem paths or assuming
how the host serves package content.

`exports.schemas`

Package-owned schema files that tools or future host features can inspect.
Visual instance settings, component props, service methods, and package
settings already have dedicated manifest fields, so use those fields for
runtime form and validation surfaces.

## Runtime APIs

Telemetry and plugin bus:

- Visuals subscribe with `context.bus.subscribe(eventName, callback)`.
- Services and connectors can also publish with `context.bus.emit(eventName,
  payload)`.
- Rocket League events use their telemetry event names.
- Package events should use the `plugin.<package-id>.*` namespace and matching
  permissions.

Registry:

- Visuals get read-only access through `context.registry.get(key)`.
- Services and connectors can read and write permitted keys.
- Registry keys are useful for latest snapshots and small shared runtime state.

Storage:

- Services and connectors can read and write permitted text storage URIs such
  as `plugin://self/state.json`.
- Storage is for package-owned persistent data, not secrets.

Service RPC:

- Visuals, services, and connectors call imported or same-package services with
  `context.services.call(ref, method, input)`.
- Inputs and outputs should be JSON-compatible because calls cross a
  host-mediated boundary.

Settings and secrets:

- Visuals receive merged package and instance settings as plain
  `context.settings`.
- Services and connectors read package settings through
  `context.settings.get(key)` and `context.settings.all()`.
- Secret settings are stored by the host and exposed only to connectors through
  `context.secrets`.

Diagnostics:

- Contexts expose `diagnostics.log`, `diagnostics.warn`, and
  `diagnostics.error`.
- Prefer diagnostics over ad hoc logging in package runtime code.

## Editor And Preview Surfaces

The editor has two distinct modes.

Editor mode mounts visuals with `context.mode === "editor"`. A visual can
provide a representative default state, and editor actions can trigger local
events such as goal, overtime, or victory. This mode is purely visual. It must
not be used as a replacement for backend services or real runtime data flow.

Preview mode mounts the same visual through its normal runtime behavior. It is
the mode to use when checking the complete chain: backend services, permitted
bus reads and publishes, registry snapshots, service RPC, settings, and real
host runtime behavior.

For event-driven animation visuals, prefer no automatic editor trigger on
mount. Show an idle or default state first, then expose explicit editor actions
for the states authors need to inspect.

## Packaging And Distribution

The normal development loop is:

```sh
npm run build
npm run check
npm run validate
npm run pack
npm run install:local
```

`validate` checks manifest shape, runtime API compatibility, package settings
schemas, built entries, page and layout template paths, configuration page
paths, and local package boundaries.

`pack` writes package hashes and a `.brlp` archive. Signed packages can also
include `signature.ed25519`. Signing and marketplace metadata help distribution
integrity, but plugin code remains untrusted and host-enforced permissions
remain the runtime boundary.
